entry.summary:
My Thinking and Approach
Introduction
In this problem, I was given a sorted linked list and asked to remove duplicate elements. Since the list is already sorted, duplicates will always appear next to each other. This made the problem easier to approach. Given a sorted linked list Remove duplicate nodes Return the updated list Initially, I thought: Store elements in a set Rebuild the linked list But this uses extra space, which is not required here. Because the list is sorted: Duplicate values are always adjacent So I only need to compare current node with the next node I decided to: Traverse the list once If current node value equals next node value: Skip the next node Otherwise: Move forward Start with curr = head
Traverse while curr and curr.next are not null If curr.data == curr.next.data: Remove duplicate by skipping node curr.next = curr.next.next Else: Move to next node class Solution { Node removeDuplicates(Node head) { Node curr = head;
while (curr != null && curr.next != null) { if (curr.data == curr.next.data) { curr.next = curr.next.next; } else { curr = curr.next; } }
return head; } }
Input: 2 → 2 → 4 → 5 Compare 2 and 2 → remove duplicate List becomes → 2 → 4 → 5
Output: 2 → 4 → 5 Time Complexity: O(n) Space Complexity: O(1) Sorted property simplifies the problem No need for extra space Pointer manipulation is important in linked lists This problem helped me understand how to efficiently handle duplicates in linked lists. It also reinforced the importance of using problem constraints (like sorted order) to simplify the solution.

My Thinking and Approach
Introduction
In this problem, I was given the head of a singly linked list and asked to reverse it. At first, I thought it might be tricky because linked lists don’t allow direct indexing like arrays. But once I understood how pointers work, the solution became clear. Given the head of a singly linked list Reverse the list Return the new head Initially, I thought: Store elements in an array Reverse the array Rebuild the linked list But this uses extra space and is not efficient. So I tried to solve it directly using pointers. I realized that: Each node points to the next node To reverse, I need to change the direction of these pointers Use three pointers: prev → previous node curr → current node next → next node Initialize: prev = null curr = head Traverse the list: Store next node → next = curr.next
Reverse pointer → curr.next = prev
Move prev forward Move curr forward At the end: prev becomes the new head class Solution { Node reverseList(Node head) { Node prev = null; Node curr = head;
while (curr != null) { Node next = curr.next; curr.next = prev; prev = curr; curr = next; }
return prev; } }
Input: 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 Reverse 1 → null Reverse 2 → 1 Reverse 3 → 2 Reverse 4 → 3 Output: 4 → 3 → 2 → 1 Input: 2 → 7 → 10 → 9 → 8 Output: 8 → 9 → 10 → 7 → 2 Time Complexity: O(n) Space Complexity: O(1) Linked lists require pointer manipulation Always store next node before changing links Iterative approach is simple and efficient This problem helped me understand how linked lists work internally. It improved my confidence in handling pointer-based problems and showed how simple logic can reverse an entire data structure efficiently.
